\"name\": name, \"email\": email})\n}\n\nNeed refactor. External behavior identical: status codes, response bodies. Note original success response: Content-Type set after possible writes? For success only. json.Encoder.Encode adds trailing newline. Body: {\"email\":\"...\",\"name\":\"...\"}? Wait map[string]string encoding: Go encoding/json sorts map keys alphabetically. For map {\"name\": name, \"email\": email}, keys email then name. So body: {\"email\":\"...\",\"name\":\"...\"}\\n. Need preserve. If we create struct with fields Name, Email, JSON output order depends struct field order. To keep identical, either use map[string]string or struct with Email first then Name? Original uses map, sorted keys: email, name. If struct type userResponse { Email string `json:\"email\"`; Name string `json:\"name\"` } output {\"email\":\"...\",\"name\":\"...\"}\\n. Good. Or use map. Need JSON encoding separated into testable function. Could have encodeUserResponse(w, user) error. But to test body, use httptest.ResponseRecorder. Need clear error handling.\n\n", "top2": [[[1596, -0.002889512339606881], [22916, -6.951129913330078]], [[1144, -3.0756469641346484e-05], [14235, -11.804487228393555]], [[4087, -0.01085151731967926], [310, -4.819540500640869]], [[1156, -0.022067252546548843], [303, -4.300877571105957]], [[579, -0.3158716559410095], [25, -2.135087490081787]], [[1622, -0.0032175034284591675], [3296, -6.731395721435547]], [[13, -0.35263097286224365], [25, -1.452318549156189]], [[14235, -0.04038091376423836], [1165, -3.9407200813293457]], [[89109, -0.25475531816482544], [7936, -2.074291467666626]], [[5805, -0.00014461133105214685], [2400, -9.2315673828125]], [[9830, -0.022629104554653168], [6795, -3.850708246231079]], [[6795, -3.0756469641346484e-05], [2952, -11.830076217651367]], [[47781, -1.307895302772522], [8387, -1.7801262140274048]], [[4475, -0.8338968753814697], [2833, -1.2312816381454468]], [[2528, -4.422762503963895e-05], [11, -10.06437873840332]], [[11, -0.00075380